for 15 year old giatti the day begins at  six in the morning  she works on the streets of delhi in her  neighborhood to the east of the city  center  it's loud here and dangerous  giotti collects plastic bottles  newspapers and cardboard what others  throw away she turns into  we money to collect trash for our family  to survive my father is ill my mother  doesn't earn anything either so my  brother and i work to feed our family  she usually works three or four hours in  the mornings  days she doesn't find enough stuff to  recycle  the material struggle is compounded by  an emotional one  people don't treat us with respect  sometimes men across the girls and shout  abuse sometimes people hit us because  they think we are stealing we're just  collecting bottles then we end up at the  police station and get abused there too  one kilogram of plastic bottles is worth  40 rupees or about half a euro  it's money giotti's family desperately  needs  they live together under a bridge on a  busy road surrounded by traffic noise  day and night  it's illegal to live here  they could be driven out any day  jonty is the first member of her family  who's trying to leave this life behind  we're nomads we have no security we're  always at the mercy of the police  it's a good thing jyoti is taking a  different path  police  after work grt goes to school  the children learn reading writing and  math but also life skills  brushing their teeth every day is part  of the program  the school is one of about a hundred in  delhi run by the ngo chetna at its  learning centers for street and working  children  chetna stands for childhood enhancement  through training and action  jyoti is also involved with barak nama  children's stories  delhi's only paper written by and for  street kids  the contributors and editors are  discussing the last edition and planning  the next one  the lead story is going to be about a  telephone hotline for children in danger  the paper was set up 10 years ago it was  children who came up with the idea  they wanted to tell their stories  the opportunity to take charge to make  our own decisions  we're independent here  nobody tells us what to put on the front  page or on the other pages  jyoti wants to see a story about  children who have managed to break their  drug habit  drugs among street kids is a serious  problem  when children see their photo in the  paper they suddenly realize that they  are somebody that's how i felt when i  first saw my picture in balagnama i  really felt good and after that i  decided to go more often to the learning  center  jyoti wants to inspire others to seize  the initiative and work for a better  life  she's visiting taimur nagar a slum of  trash collectors  she wants to impress on these kids how  important it is to get an education  they've written poems and jyoti will  have them published in balochnama  she can imagine working as a journalist  when she's grown up  i want to give people a voice  street kids do all kinds of incredible  things  and despite all their problems they want  to give their lives meaning  and i want to write about that  for children in taiwan  giatti is already something of a hero a  role model  there is no easy way to escape poverty  but giotti has the determination to try  and someday  leave behind the world of collecting  rubbish to make a living
 

